## Major componenets of xLaDe

We explain the components with respect to current implementation.
Future versions may need to edit this.

The various main components of xLaDe are as follows:

1. CLI layer
2. Experiment layer
3. Tooling layer
4. Lean layer
5. Workflow layer
6. Docs layer

---

## CLI Layer

It includes the CLI provided in xlade/ and bin/.
xlade/ is pip installable, while bin/ is for manual invokation.

It provides user a terminal interface with some commands so that user can easily perform many actions such as:

- initialising and cleaning workspace
- selecting a mode
- checking list of and running experiments
- viewing status, metrics, and other info

It is the intended way to use xLaDe and is our main focus or main product.
xLaDe is modular and simple. Most commands have their own module

    |-------|  primary usage   |-------|
    | User  | ---------------> |  CLI  |
    |-------|                  |-------|

---

## Experiment Layer

It includes the experiments/, projects/, and scripts/experiments/.
Experiments are the projects based on Lean (or anything user decides) which are run by xLaDe.
They are the main artifacts of xLaDe.
To know more about experiments, please read the experiments/README.

Experiments are separated from CLI and have their own structure and usage such as:

- record toolchain and environment metadata
- versioned commit for reproducibility
- easy to create and low overhead
- no restriction on what can be an experiment

Experiment scripts are kept under scripts/experiments/ and provide a central collection of scripts.
The main reason behind this separation is taht if two or more experiment need same script, so that it can be used easily.
This removes problem to rewrite same script and reduces friction.

Projects are the experiments which have been initialised but not completed yet.
They are intended for the users to take help from community to improve and complete them over time.

An experiment run via the CLI and uses script for the completion if needed.

    |-------|       runs       |-------------|    uses          |------------|
    | User  | ---------------> | Experiment  | ---------------> |    CLI     |
    |-------|                  |-------------|                  |  Scripts   |
                                                                | Lean,etc.  |
                                                                |------------|

---

# Tooling Layer

It includes the metrics, modes, scripts/core, and tools/.
This layer contains the extra tools and helping material which can be useful for the projects.
The several uses of tooling layer are following:

- Controlling execution of experiments via mode
- Custom scripts as per experiment
- External tools for more capabilities
- Metrics for measuring the performance and comparison

The tooling layer helps xLaDe bring more features and customise it as per the users' needs.
However, the separation of tools from core of xLaDe makes it better to not have them mandatorily if they are not required by the experiment.
As some experiments may not need tools, so it depends on context.

    |-------|  primary usage   |-------|       uses for         |------------|
    | User  | ---------------> |  CLI  | ---------------------> |   Tooling  |
    |-------|                  |-------|     extra features     |------------|

---

# Lean Layer

It includes the lean-core/, lakefile.lean, lean toolchains, and other lean 4 things.
xLaDe is dependent on Lean 4 for proof verification.
However, we don't improve, customise or change anything in Lean 4.

This xLaDe project uses latest stable version of Lean 4.
However, every experiment defines and uses its own Lean toolchain.
This separation keeps experiments and xlade toolchains different and doesn't pollute them.

---

# Workflow Layer

It includes the .github/workflows, tests/, and scripts/extra/.
These are provided for the betterment of the xLaDe project itself.
Users are not primarily intended to use them as they don't directly help in users' work.

Github workflows offer help in checking the integrity of Lean 4 kernel, formatting of code, CI, CD, etc.

Tests are essential parts of xLaDe and used for testing various behaviour and mock uses of xLaDe to know of any problems in advance.
However, tests don't mean that there are 0 bugs and there may be gaps.

scripts/extra provide scripts for miscellaneous purpose, which aren't related directly to core of xLaDe or experiments.
However, these still need to be relevant to the xLaDe project.
This ensures that the separation is more precise and helpful to others.

    |-------|  project helper  |-------------|
    | xLaDe | ---------------> |  workflows  | 
    |-------|                  |-------------|

    |-------|     no usage     |-------------|
    | User  | xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx |  workflows  | 
    |-------|                  |-------------|

---

# Docs Layer

It includes the security/, docs/, README.md, LICENSE and other documentation files.

We treat the security and documentation as equally important as the programming part.
Our security/ directory contains the trust model, threat model and security policy.
In addition, we release DCVEs (Decentralised Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) in the security directory for important findings after update.
This ensures that security measures are updated together and are separated from normal documentation.

Documentation of xLaDe is included in the docs/ directory.
It also includes introductory articles for learning more about the xLaDe.
Documentation is regularly updated and revised for accuracy and reliability.

This makes xLaDe better in the helping aspect.

---

Each layer above has its own role, however xLaDe work by combining all of these layers and making them work in coherence.
This architecture may change in future versions.

